1. AWS Regions

An AWS Region is a separate geographic area where AWS has its infrastructure.

Each Region contains multiple Availability Zones.

AWS Region
    │
    ├── Availability Zone 1
    ├── Availability Zone 2
    └── Availability Zone 3
Why use different Regions?
🌍 Serve users closer to their location
⚡ Reduce network latency
🔒 Meet data-location requirements
💰 Potentially optimize costs
🛡️ Improve disaster recovery


2. Availability Zones (AZs)

An Availability Zone consists of one or more isolated data centers within an AWS Region.

Multiple AZs allow applications to remain available even if one AZ experiences a failure.

          AWS Region
       ┌───────────────┐
       │ AZ-1   AZ-2   │
       │  🖥️     🖥️     │
       │               │
       │     AZ-3      │
       │      🖥️       │
       └───────────────┘
Main benefits
High availability
Fault isolation
Redundancy
Disaster recovery

3. Edge / Local Zones

AWS also provides infrastructure closer to specific population centers to reduce latency for applications that need users to access resources quickly.

Goal: Bring computing resources closer to end users.

Key Takeaways
🌍 Region → Geographic AWS location
🏢 Availability Zone → Isolated infrastructure within a Region
🔄 Multiple AZs → Higher availability & fault tolerance
⚡ Edge/Local infrastructure → Resources closer to users
📉 Closer infrastructure can help reduce latency
